    {
      "filename": "Flooding along the Kitakami River, Japan (5532218010).jpg",
      "title": "File:Flooding along the Kitakami River, Japan (5532218010).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Flooding_along_the_Kitakami_River%2C_Japan_%285532218010%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flooding_along_the_Kitakami_River,_Japan_(5532218010).jpg",
      "width": 720,
      "height": 965,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
      "description": "NASA image top acquired March 14 and bottom acquired January 16, 2011.\nIn the wake of the magnitude 9.0 earthquake and resulting tsunami that struck northeastern Japan in March 2011, ocean waters flooded croplands and settlements lining the Kitakami River. The Advanced Spaceborne Thermal Emission and Reflection Radiometer (ASTER) on NASA’s Terra satellite captured these false-color images before and after the tsunami. The top image is from March 14, 2011, and the bottom is from January 16, 2011.\nThe images combine infrared, red, and green wavelengths of light to form a false-color image that distinguishes between muddy water and land. Water is blue. Buildings and paved surfaces appear in shades of blue-gray. Fallow fields appear in shades of beige and brown. Vegetation is red, and the brighter the red, the more robust the vegetation. (Brighter shades of red in March and duller shades in January result largely from the difference in season.)\nIn the March image, water has spilled over the banks both north and south of the river. Although agricultural fields appear to have escaped the flooding farther inland (image left), some fields closer to the ocean have seemingly disappeared into the sea. North of the Kitakami, floodwaters extend far enough inland to create what looks like a parallel river. Near the coast, only the rugged peaks rising above the floodplains have escaped inundation.\nOn March 15, 2011 (early on March 16 in Japan), NHK World reported that the number of dead and missing likely exceeded 10,000. While police reported 3,373 confirmed deaths, 7,558 people remained missing. More than 440,000 people had sought refuge in temporary shelters.\nNASA Earth Observatory image by Robert Simmon and Jesse Allen, using data from the GSFC/METI/ERSDAC/JAROS, and U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team. Caption by Michon Scott.\nInstrument: Terra - ASTER\nCredit: NASA Earth Observatory\nNASA Goddard Space Flight Center enables NASA’s mission through four scientific endeavors: Earth Science, Heliophysics, Solar System Exploration, and Astrophysics. Goddard plays a leading role in NASA’s accomplishments by contributing compelling scientific knowledge to advance the Agency’s mission.\nFollow us on Twitter\n\nJoin us on Facebook",
      "dateCreated": "2011-03-16 10:19",
